WEBVTT

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:10.100
When you think about searching for a piece of lost media, it's often fueled by the idea that

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.960
you can eventually find the content and archive it, so it won't become lost again.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.430
And in most cases, that's exactly what happens.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:24.420
There's usually someone associated with production, a re-upload on another website,

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.970
or even a fan in a community that has something they're not aware people are looking for.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.930
for. However, that doesn't apply to every topic, and in some cases, no matter how many people you

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:45.320
might contact on a search, sometimes that piece of lost media doesn't exist anymore. We're not

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.800
talking about content like old silent films and internet videos that nobody can find. Those could

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:58.130
still exist somewhere. I'm talking about content that has pretty much been confirmed to not exist

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.860
anymore. Whether the only cop

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.070
was never saved, or the master files wiped, these topics are very unlikely to ever resurface,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:11.000
as stated by the people and companies who worked on them.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.400
Adult Swim original programming is something I've become more interested in over the years,

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.620
as my view of animation has increased, and also as I've generally become older and more

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:28.980
interested in what I wasn't allowed to watch growing up.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:29.970
Yeah.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.500
Yeah, I had those friends who were into South Park and Family Guy in elementary school,

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:40.670
but it was a long time before I was allowed to watch Adult Swim, and even once I was able

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:45.510
to watch it, I only caught the anime and wasn't interested in anything else.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:51.120
But now it's a different story, and I'm eager to explore the creative and sometimes

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.120
strange world of Adult Swim animation, which includes the lost media side of things too.

00:01:58.270 --> 00:01:59.810
There's been some notable

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.380
examples that have been discussed over the years, like the Aqua Teen Hunger

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.950
Force Boston episode, which is probably one of the most famous. But then there's

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:15.960
my absolute favorite kind of Adult Swim lost media, the Lost Pilots. There are

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:20.900
quite a few Lost Pilots from Adult Swim shows that have never resurfaced in full,

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:27.340
but one in particular that not only has an interesting story, but it's also one

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.840
that I didn't know existed, so it counts.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.200
as another new discovery for me too.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.730
This is the pilot for 12-ounce Mouse,

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:41.170
a comedy cartoon from the mid-2000s, best known for its simplistic art style.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:46.110
I remember coming across clips of this show on YouTube a few years ago,

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.150
and became very interested in the concept,

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:51.970
but never got around to watching the series.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:57.310
However, something that I'll never see is that lost pilot,

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:59.530
because it doesn't actually exist anymore.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:05.600
In a 2021 interview with the 12 Ounce Mouse fan site, Corndog Central,

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:10.640
series editor John Breston made mention of an early pilot

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:16.000
that very few crew members who worked on the series were even aware of, stating,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.310
I wish I saved the pilot episode. Only Mayolaro and I know it. It's lost to time now.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:29.820
I kind of hate myself for not documenting this history. It's so rare. Our SVP Keith Crawford

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:36.520
was the voice of golden joe and he was mouse's boss for real it's gone now forever i hate that

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:43.500
i didn't document it it was also mentioned that the pilot was never meant to air and several of

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:50.780
its assets were made purely to sell the show to adult swim eventually another pilot was created

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:57.670
and titled hired which became the official first episode of the series as well and originally aired

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:58.750
in 2005.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:06.000
year after the original pilot was made. It's really unfortunate that nothing from the original vision

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.680
of the show was saved, and it's even more unfortunate that there's nothing we can do to

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.860
recover content from it, since the only people who had access to it confirmed they don't anymore.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.620
This would be like if Van Partable, the only person who has a copy of the Johnny Bravo pilot

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:29.960
Messo Blues, lost his only copy years ago, or didn't have it archived.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.010
it would be gone forever. I'm also sure there wouldn't be any other copies in

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:40.580
existence since a second pilot was made once the series was picked up, and that's

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:45.600
exactly like Johnny Bravo 2. The What A Cartoon pilot is what Cartoon Network

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.910
would have in their archives, not Meso Blues. The details about what happened

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:56.370
to it specifically are not discussed, so I'm not sure if it was just misplaced or

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.880
deleted but unless another copy is found by John

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:09.250
Breaston, or the series creator, Matt Maialaro, it's gone forever. It's sort of become a recurring

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.860
joke when it comes to pieces of lost media that are particularly bad looking or awful,

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:21.550
that the content is so bad, it should stay lost forever, rather than being found. And by pure

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:27.860
coincidence, that's exactly the scenario that led to one production pretty much being erased

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:29.900
from existence, for its poor

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:37.210
production quality, and accidents on the set. What's more is that all of the disasters happened

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:43.320
live, so it's not often you hear about this kind of thing, and that makes it both more interesting

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.850
and hilarious to me. I'll be the first to admit I'm not a big fan of Thomas the Tank Engine,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:56.300
or the lost media surrounding the series, and there's actually a surprising amount of it if

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.980
you do your research. The last time I really thought about Tom

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.380
Thomas was back when I was a kid, and had a whole train table set up, with tracks and

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:06.640
characters from the show.

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:13.570
But a few years ago, everyone seemed to jump on the Lost Thomas pilot, and that seemed

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:15.830
to be a pretty big interest for the community.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.650
However, I've come across a piece of Thomas Lost Media that is perhaps more interesting

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:28.690
than that, and one that I really wish I could see, but sadly, I don't think we ever will.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:37.160
The series of Thomas the Tank Engine that most people are familiar with is the 1984 TV series,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.240
but predating that is a broadcast of The Sad Story of Henry,

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.030
the first adaptation of the Railway Series books from 1953.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:53.870
This was handled by the BBC after approaching series editor Eric Marriott

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:59.970
for permission to adapt two stories for television, which was approved, and work began on...

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:07.400
project. Train models, track layouts, and scripting were all completed in a month, which was really

00:07:07.400 --> 00:07:12.310
quick for what was expected to be a complex production, but it was still scheduled to be

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:20.700
broadcast live on June 14th, 1953, which is when things didn't go as planned. Recollections state

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:27.260
that the broadcast started off well, despite the engines being jerky in some spots, but it really

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.980
went awry when the lead engine derailed.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.470
failed, and a human hand had to pick it up and place it back on the track, which was

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:40.880
an unwelcome surprise, and broke the illusion of it being set in the Thomas universe.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:48.240
Following the broadcast, newspapers took notice of the overall poor production, and gave the

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:53.610
production poor reviews, which was followed by a list of revisions that had to be made

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:59.970
for the second broadcast, that was scheduled for June 28th, though this one would end up

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.480
being cancelled and never happened, making the original derailment broadcast the one and only

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:13.680
to have been made, and it hasn't been seen since. It's not surprising this ended up being the case,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:20.140
considering the BBC doesn't have very good archives of their content, and have even wiped their

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:27.460
archives before. So, it's believed that even if a recording did exist at one point, it no longer

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.810
does. Though since

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.530
there was so much bad publicity with the train derailment, I'm sure it's not something they even

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:41.740
wanted to save. It was also a live performance, and the era of home recording was not around,

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:48.120
so it's unlikely that anyone was able to save it privately. Though according to the Lost Media

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:53.720
Wiki article, there have been claims that people have obtained clips or recalled ways in which

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:59.700
it could have been recorded. Apparently, these are not factual, and the real broadcast has never

00:08:59.700 --> 00:08:59.900
been

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:06.930
unearthed, with the only primary sources we have being the newspaper reviews. While doing research

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:14.160
for the video, I did come across a few pictures that get passed around and are claimed to be from

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:20.760
the live broadcast, but even these have been debunked, with their origin being from a 1980s

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:27.880
event in the UK of fan-made and modified Thomas Train models. It's hard to believe there hasn't

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:29.880
even been an authentic photo from the

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.580
set or rehearsals that depict the setup or could give us an idea of how the models looked.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:42.490
Unfortunately, this is one of those instances where the older the piece of lost media is,

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.120
the less likely it is to still exist.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:54.140
When it comes to lost media, most of the time the media in question becomes lost by accident,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:59.980
and it's not often that creators intentionally make content unavailable for viewers.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.590
after it was made. So only a special set of circumstances would allow for that to

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:13.370
happen, and this is a really interesting story when it did. I came across this

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:18.610
article on the Lost Media Wiki a while ago, but forgot about it for a long time,

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:25.100
probably because I never even heard about this show before. This is the Fun

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:29.450
Day Paw Pet Show, which is apparently one of the longest-running internet

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:29.810
at shows.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.470
in existence, with nearly 800 episodes spanning 18 years of content.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:41.460
Before researching this topic for the video, I hadn't even seen a second's worth of

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:42.640
content from the show.

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:49.550
So after looking it up, it's a comedic puppet show that uses animal characters in humorous

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:50.510
skits and banter.

00:10:51.240 --> 00:10:57.630
I kinda liked it, so it might be surprising to hear that this playful style changed completely

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.800
for one day, which

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.270
never be seen again. It was on September 11, 2001, when the cast of the show put together

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:12.670
a special episode to comfort those affected by the tragedies of the attacks that day.

00:11:13.990 --> 00:11:18.960
Recollections state that the episode was very heartwarming, with some of the best moments

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:25.350
from the show, which is quite a feat for a series that is centered on humor. While the

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:27.310
Fun Day Pawpets show recorded

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:29.350
their episodes for download afterwards,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:34.900
This episode in particular was confirmed to have not been recorded for the archives,

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:39.700
on the official website, and to this day, it's never resurfaced.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:45.390
The Lost Media Wiki article mentions it might have been made unavailable

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.570
because of irregular guests and licensing issues,

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:54.140
but I think that was less of an issue, and it was more the content of the episode,

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.470
which is also stated in the article.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.980
The cast probably felt that it was more important to...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.060
keep the content of the episode private to those who watched it at the time, and the

00:12:05.060 --> 00:12:08.340
description on the website even states it was impromptu.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:15.170
It's likely that a recording was never made, so the only way one could exist is if someone

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:16.710
at home recorded it themselves.

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:22.450
But aside from recording software being primitive at the time, I don't think there's any

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:27.170
reason why someone would have recorded it, especially if the main episodes were being

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.150
uploaded to their site anyway at the time.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.400
Though there was a bit of confusion with the episode's resurfacing,

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.720
when a video was uploaded to YouTube on April 1st, 2017,

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.700
which claimed to be footage of the special,

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:44.060
but was actually from the following week's episode,

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.560
and was available for download anyway,

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:50.720
immediately dismissing the possibility of it being the lost one.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.820
Given the content of the episode,

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:58.060
and how the crew didn't feel like it needed to be archived even back then,

00:12:58.340 --> 00:12:59.960
I have to agree that I don't...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.950
think it's something that needs to be found, but will likely stay lost to time for that reason.

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:13.390
I feel like it's been a while since I've talked about video game lost media from a big series,

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:19.860
so it'd be nice to sprinkle some into this video. And in fact, we're going to be talking about a

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:25.730
topic that I've had on my video list for a long time, though unfortunately it's not from the

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:29.150
Dead or Alive series, but rather a different Koei Tecmo game.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.060
I would like to come back to Dead or Alive in another video, though there isn't a whole

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:39.990
lot of lost media to talk about from the series, but it's interesting enough that I'd like

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.430
to dedicate some time to it.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:47.410
But for now, we're going to be talking about Ninja Gaiden, which is a series I only got

00:13:47.410 --> 00:13:48.150
into recently.

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:54.660
I wouldn't say I'm a big fan of the series, but when I bought an original Xbox last year,

00:13:54.950 --> 00:13:59.980
I was looking for some exclusives to play on the console, and Ninja Gaiden was one that

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.650
was recommended. Though, I always have a hard time deciding where to start when playing a

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:10.800
new series for the first time, so I wasn't sure if the Xbox Ones were the right place

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:16.300
to start, since there were other collections on PlayStation and even Switch that I could

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:23.040
get more easily. However, the fans were nearly in agreement that Ninja Gaiden Black, on the

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:29.580
original Xbox, was one of the best titles in the series, so I did decide to start there.

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:34.800
but checked to make sure this game wasn't bundled in some kind of collection that was cheaper.

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.280
And to my surprise, it wasn't.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:41.460
I went through all the re-releases that existed for the series,

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:44.650
and Ninja Gaiden Black wasn't on any of them,

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:49.740
despite the fans stating it was one of the best, which seemed very weird to me.

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:54.500
But after doing some more research, there was a surprising explanation

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.250
that stated why the game had never been re-released before.

00:14:59.140 --> 00:14:59.980
When Team Ninja-

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.200
was putting together the Ninja Gaiden Master Collection, they made a statement that the

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:12.990
original source code for Ninja Gaiden Black and Ninja Gaiden 2 was lost, meaning they had to

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:19.530
resort to using the Sigma re-releases of the game for the new collection. What's even more interesting

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:26.410
is the head of Team Ninja, Fumihiko Yasuda, explained that the reason we chose these three

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:29.900
titles for the collection is because they represent the latest

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:35.410
installments of the numbered series, and are also the most polished versions, featuring

00:15:35.410 --> 00:15:42.090
many additional modes and costumes. Which didn't sit well with fans, and it's believed this statement

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:48.190
was only made to save face, as the originals were received much better than the Sigma remakes.

00:15:48.940 --> 00:15:54.140
I don't have enough experience with the series to make a judgment on which versions are the

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:59.380
definitive ones, but it seems like Black on the original Xbox was the best choice.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.060
So that was the one I ended up going with.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:04.000
And while I haven't beaten it yet,

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:09.300
I'll be thinking about how this release is the only way I'll be able to experience the game.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:13.560
And in some sort of way, I'm playing a piece of lost media.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.520
That's right guys, I went there.

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:22.000
I'm talking about another piece of Sega Saturn Lost Media,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:26.150
even after I talked about High School Terror Story in my last video.

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:29.970
But this topic is much more interesting for the mis-

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.460
mysterious and unconfirmed story surrounding this title, and it's from one of my favorite genres of

00:16:36.460 --> 00:16:43.600
all time, shmups. The Saturn itself is a great console for shmups, and that's pretty much what

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:48.930
got me to collect for it in the first place. And right now, I have a pretty good number of fun and

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:55.270
harder to get games, but there's one I've been wanting for a while, and have yet to get. This

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:59.960
This game is called Blast Wind, and for a long time, I didn't even know what it was.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.360
know the game existed, because it's nowhere near as popular as other shmups on the console,

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:11.840
despite being made by Technosoft, who has become very well known for Thunder Force and Hyper Duel.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:17.670
But the story about the game doesn't end with how obscure or how difficult it can be to find.

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:24.400
It begins with the fact that it shouldn't really exist at all. This information comes from

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:29.980
Hardcore Gaming 101, and is one of the only places online that I've

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.280
ever seen this info documented, and it states that despite being released in 1997 exclusively

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:44.030
on the Sega Saturn, Blastwind's origins date back to 1993, when it was rumored to

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:50.470
exist as an arcade game based on some industry publications that advertised its release.

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:57.270
This information comes from MAME developer Shimapong, and was backed by another claim

00:17:57.270 --> 00:17:59.850
from PCB collector show who

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.260
stated a prototype for the game was field-tested in Sasebo, the hometown of Technosoft. While the

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:14.150
PCB for this release was claimed to still exist, it gets more interesting when we look at a magazine

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:20.920
that mentioned a completely different Technosoft game called Inazuma Saber that was planned for

00:18:20.920 --> 00:18:27.240
release in 1994. But this is where the mystery begins, because while there has never been any

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:29.980
other documentation of Inazuma Saban.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:37.840
outside of this magazine, in 2001, a source was discovered that a user claimed to have played

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:45.080
Inazuma Saber at a test location and described the game as a lighthearted revamp of Blast Wind,

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:51.660
with comedic dialogue spoken between the pilots. But this is the most detail we've ever heard about

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:58.980
the game. No screenshots, no other sightings, and no proof that PCB boards still exist have ever been

00:18:58.980 --> 00:18:59.320
found.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:07.390
It's not even confirmed that a PCB still exists for Blast Wind, even though it allegedly was in

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:13.180
existence at one point. But as stated in the Hardcore Gaming article, until a Technosoft

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:18.880
member comes forward and explains these mysteries, we'll probably never get an answer on them.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:25.960
Though it is believed that Blast Wind was ported to the Saturn because Technosoft needed the money,

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:29.130
and shortly after, the company went out of business.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:35.780
we don't know why the arcade version was cancelled in the first place, or reworked into Inazuma

00:19:35.780 --> 00:19:41.340
Saber, only for that game to be cancelled as well. It's kind of a really complicated

00:19:41.340 --> 00:19:47.270
mystery when you consider it's a Japanese exclusive Saturn game, and not a cartoon pilot

00:19:47.270 --> 00:19:52.670
or something with a bigger appeal. I definitely don't run into these kinds of things very

00:19:52.670 --> 00:19:58.360
often with the console, so it's really fun looking into them, and as a shmup fan, I think

00:19:58.360 --> 00:19:59.960
I think I'd put Inazuma Saber

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.500
on my list of lost games I'd like to resurface, along with Dodonpachi campaign version.

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:10.500
This also brings up an interesting point about international lost media,

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.820
since I feel like if the mystery was from the US,

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:20.330
you'd have arcade enthusiasts all over the topic, making contacts and being able to get a board.

00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:26.360
But with the game being exclusive to Japan, from a Japanese company that was left in the 90s,

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:29.960
related to a console exclusive, its appeal is much more.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:36.150
smaller, and it's really hard to research being outside of Japan, which isn't the first time this

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:42.310
has happened either. It's interesting to consider what people decide they want to document and what

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:48.450
they don't, whether that's intentional or something they regret later on. But it does make you consider

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:54.880
how lucky we are that so much content can even be found again. What if every cartoon pilot was

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:59.540
never archived by the show staff, or all internet content was taken down.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.240
after it originally aired.

00:21:02.110 --> 00:21:07.580
This makes finding lost media even more important, and it can give you a better appreciation

00:21:08.050 --> 00:21:10.430
for what the community has been able to save.
